1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,460
A desk breaking in Control

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:06,140
combines large planks, small fragments and dust.

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,380
To create this response,

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,460
calculating only the moment of impact is not enough.

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:11,740
Materials and fracture locations,

6
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,360
along with their connections, need preparation.

7
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,120
In this office, shots are followed by

8
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,560
flying fragments and dust.

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:19,940
One impact is shown through

10
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:22,040
both large changes and small effects.

11
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:24,020
The developers considered layers from whole objects

12
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:27,140
to fragments and smaller particles.

13
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:28,660
Large pieces change the shape,

14
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:31,480
while small fragments and dust fill out the impact.

15
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,560
Even the smallest dust need not be treated

16
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:36,660
as an equally expensive simulated body.

17
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,600
When a large panel falls in the model,

18
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,400
the desk’s outline changes.

19
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,160
Nearby chips and dust

20
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,880
make the impact location easier to read.

21
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,500
Material data and rules reduced repetitive work.

22
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,740
Wood and glass should not fracture identically.

23
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:53,700
Define material-appropriate fracture methods,

24
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,500
then build a hierarchy of large and small fragments.

25
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:58,860
Imagine splitting a wooden panel into long pieces,

26
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:02,040
while a glass panel uses a different fracture pattern.

27
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,200
Preparing that difference as a material rule

28
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,840
avoids repeating the work for every desk.

29
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,660
Pieces would scatter immediately if they were only separated.

30
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:12,940
Prepare connections between pieces and their strength.

31
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:16,300
Send the prepared pieces and physics data to the engine.

32
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:19,160
Then control what breaks away and what remains attached.

33
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:20,500
Think of prepared puzzle pieces

34
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,860
held together by weak glue.

35
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:25,380
Connections near an impact can release

36
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,040
while other connections remain intact.

37
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:31,400
An impact breaks connections and physical pieces move.

38
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:35,340
Collisions and breakage also generate chips and dust.

39
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:37,700
Trigger effects appropriate to the material.

40
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:41,260
Large pieces and small effects communicate the impact together.

41
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:42,780
Watch the large panel fall

42
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,880
as dust spreads from the same location.

43
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:47,400
Physical fragments change the shape;

44
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,200
particles fill out the impression of a break.

45
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,460
Marks left on surfaces also enrich the destruction.

46
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,820
But a mark drawn on a surface

47
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,320
is different from an actual geometric hole.

48
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:00,500
Control’s destruction links prepared fragments, bonds,

49
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:03,980
runtime physics and smaller visual traces.

50
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:06,140
Painting a dark mark on a wall

51
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,980
differs from a piece of wall falling away.

52
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:10,780
Marks, fragments, and released connections

53
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:12,920
work together to create a richer response.
